            <ArticleTitle>Intra-coronary stents--extending the role of angioplasty in angina--initial experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>102-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) is a widely successful revascularisation procedure. The restenosis rate has not significantly improved, despite the addition of many therapeutic agents. We describe the role of intra-coronary stents in the management of recurrent occlusion, restenosis and intracoronary dissection. We describe and discuss the problems associated with placement of the stent. The intracoronary stent has a successful adjuvant role to play with angioplasty in reducing both the elective and emergency requirements for coronary artery bypass grafting.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
